const lesson = {
  title: 'Perceptrons, Neurons & Activation Functions',
  readTime: '~75 min read + 50 min practice',
  hasIntegratedGuide: true,
  content: () => <div className="perceptron-lesson lesson-flow">
 <LessonIntro prerequisites={<>Read weighted sums and matrix shapes; review <a href="/learn/math-foundations/vectors-matrices-tensor-operations">vectors and matrices</a> and <a href="/learn/math-foundations/algebra-functions-exponentials-logarithms">functions and exponentials</a>. Derivatives are introduced locally as slopes, from a measured nudge.</>} sections={[['1-a-neuron-is-a-weighted-question', 'Trace a neuron'], ['2-a-perceptron-learns-a-boundary-from-mistakes', 'Learn from mistakes'], ['3-hidden-neurons-change-what-the-model-can-express', 'Construct XOR'], ['4-activation-functions-shape-values-and-sensitivities', 'Inspect local sensitivity'], ['5-train-a-small-network-on-real-handwriting', 'Compare real digit fits'], ['7-deeper-branches-smooth-gates-expressive-capacity-and-cost', 'Deeper branches'], ['8-practice-explain-compute-diagnose', 'Independent practice']]}>Score a tiny image with a weighted template, watch a perceptron learn a boundary from its mistakes, build hidden features that solve XOR, and compare activation choices in one controlled real-data experiment.</LessonIntro>

<Prose>{"A handwriting recognizer receives numbers, not the idea of a “7.” Each image arrives as a grid of pixel intensities, and the program must turn that grid into evidence for each digit. A neural network does this with many small, identical-looking calculations: weigh some numbers, add them up, transform the total, and pass the result on. Training adjusts the weights."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"One such calculation is an "}<strong>{"artificial neuron"}</strong>{". The biological name is only an analogy; the object we build is an ordinary function you can compute by hand. This lesson builds it in the order you need it: first one neuron answering one question, then a rule that learns its weights from mistakes, then the limit of a single neuron and how hidden neurons remove it, and finally the activation choices that make larger networks trainable."}</Prose>
<Prose opening="route"><strong>{"First pass:"}</strong>{" follow §§1–6 with Investigations A–E and practices 1–5, 9 and 10. You will score a pixel template, step through perceptron training, build an XOR network by hand and read a small digit recognizer's results. §7 and practices 6–8 are deeper branches: smooth gates, approximation theory and resource accounting, useful but not needed for the next lesson. Allow about 65–80 minutes for reading and worked examples, plus 40–55 minutes for practice and the CPU experiment; the deeper branch adds 25–40 minutes."}</Prose>
<Prose>Each section starts from a question the previous one leaves open. Pictures come first: look at each one, then read the explanation that walks through it. The investigations show their current result as soon as they appear; change one control at a time and watch the numbers and the picture move together.</Prose>

<H2>{"1. A neuron is a weighted question"}</H2>
<Prose>Start smaller than a digit. Take a 3 × 3 image where each pixel is either inked (1) or blank (0), and one yes/no question: <strong>is there a vertical stroke down the middle column?</strong></Prose>
<Prose>One simple way to answer is a <strong>template</strong>: a grid of numbers, one per pixel, saying how that pixel should count. Multiply each pixel by its template number, add the nine products, and compare the total with a threshold. Here is a template for this question, applied to three images.</Prose>
<TemplateExampleFigure />
<Prose>Walk through the clean vertical bar first. Its three middle pixels are inked and their template numbers are +1, so each contributes +1; every other pixel is blank and contributes 0 whatever its template number. The products add to 3. The template gives the middle column +1 because those pixels support the answer “yes,” and gives every other pixel −1 because an inked pixel anywhere else argues against a clean stroke. That is why the stray pixel in the second image costs 1 (total 3 − 1 = 2), and why the horizontal bar, with only one middle-column pixel, totals 1 − 2 = −1. A blank pixel never matters, whatever its template number.</Prose>
<Prose>How large must the total be before we answer yes? That requirement is a separate number, the <strong>bias</strong> b, added to the total. With b = −2 the clean bar scores 3 − 2 = 1, which is above 0, so the answer is yes. The stray-pixel image scores 2 − 2 = 0, and the rule is “yes only when the score is above 0,” so this exact tie answers no. The horizontal bar scores −1 − 2 = −3: no.</Prose>
<FlowCheck prompt="Keep the vertical template. Which biases make the bar-plus-stray-pixel image answer yes while the horizontal bar still answers no?">
<Prose>The stray-pixel image has contributions 3 − 1 = 2, so its score is 2 + b and needs b &gt; −2. The horizontal bar has 1 − 2 = −1, so it answers no while −1 + b ≤ 0, that is b ≤ 1. Any bias with −2 &lt; b ≤ 1 works, for example −1.5. The clean bar (3 + b) then also answers yes. Loosening the bias accepts messier strokes; it does not change what each pixel contributes.</Prose>
</FlowCheck>
<Prose><strong>Investigation A — score your own image.</strong> The grids below start from the stray-pixel image at its tie. Click a pixel to ink or blank it, click a weight to cycle it through +1, 0 and −1, move the bias, or switch to a template for a horizontal stroke. Every change recomputes the products, the score and the answer. Try drawing the horizontal bar under the vertical template, then switch the template.</Prose>
<PixelTemplateLab />

<H3>The same calculation, drawn as an artificial neuron</H3>
<Prose>Everything the template did is the work of one <strong>artificial neuron</strong>: multiply each input by a weight, add the products and a bias, then decide what to send on. Nine inputs make a crowded drawing, so here is the same kind of neuron with two inputs: x₁ = 2 and x₂ = −1, weights 1.5 and −2, and bias −1. Inputs can be any measured numbers, including negative ones, or outputs of other neurons.</Prose>
<NeuronAnatomyFigure />
<Prose>Follow the arrows from the inputs to the output. The cyan circles are the <strong>inputs</strong>, supplied data for this example. Each arrow is a <strong>connection</strong>, and the amber number on it is that connection's <strong>weight</strong>: the arrow delivers its input multiplied by the weight, so x₁ arrives as 1.5 × 2 = 3 and x₂ as −2 × (−1) = 2. The first white box, Σ + b, is an operation: Σ means “add up,” so it adds the arriving products and the bias, 3 + 2 − 1 = 4. That total is the neuron's <strong>score</strong>, written z. The second white box applies a rule to the score; here it is the template's rule, 1 if z &gt; 0 and 0 otherwise, so the violet output circle holds a = 1. The dotted outline encloses one neuron, opened up to show its two operations.</Prose>
<Prose>The colours separate three kinds of number. Weights and the bias (amber) are the neuron's <strong>parameters</strong>: training changes only these. Inputs (cyan) are supplied by the data. The score and the output (violet) are computed every time. Move x₂ or the bias under the diagram. Because x₂'s weight is negative, making x₂ more negative raises the score; the bias shifts the score without looking at any input. Keep x₂ at −1 and lower the bias until the output switches to 0; then check where that happens against the score 3 + 2 + b. The second box stays the template's step rule in this drawing, so the output can only be 0 or 1. The figure after the equations below sends this neuron's score of 4 through step, ReLU and sigmoid side by side, and Investigation E in §4 lets you switch the rule yourself.</Prose>
<Prose>{"In symbols, with "}<Math>{"d"}</Math>{" inputs (nine pixels for the template, two in the drawing), the neuron computes"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"z=w_1x_1+w_2x_2+\\dots+w_dx_d+b=w^\\top x+b,\\qquad a=\\phi(z)."}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"Each product "}<Math>{"w_ix_i"}</Math>{" is one arrow's contribution, and the sum is the Σ box. The notation "}<Math>{"w^\\top x"}</Math>{" is the same sum of products written as a dot product. The weighted sum plus bias is called an "}<strong>{"affine"}</strong>{" calculation; libraries call the layer that computes it “linear” even when a bias is present. The symbol "}<Math>{"\\phi"}</Math>{" names the rule in the second box, the "}<strong>{"activation function"}</strong>{"; "}<Math>{"z"}</Math>{" is also called the "}<strong>{"preactivation"}</strong>{" and "}<Math>{"a"}</Math>{" the neuron's "}<strong>{"activation"}</strong>{" or output."}</Prose>

<H3>{"What to do with the score: the activation"}</H3>
<Prose>{"The template needed a yes/no answer, so its activation was a hard step. Other jobs need other rules. To pass on “how much evidence, ignoring any shortfall,” use "}<strong>{"ReLU"}</strong>{", "}<Math>{"\\max(0,z)"}</Math>{". For a smooth value squeezed between 0 and 1, use "}<strong>{"sigmoid"}</strong>{", "}<Math>{"1/(1+e^{-z})"}</Math>{", which is 0.5 at "}<Math>{"z=0"}</Math>{" and approaches 1 for large positive scores and 0 for large negative ones. Here is the drawn neuron's score, "}<Math>{"z=4"}</Math>{", under each of the three."}</Prose>
<ActivationChoiceFigure />
<Prose>The score is the same in every panel; only the rule in the second box changed. The step outputs 1, ReLU passes the 4 through unchanged, and sigmoid gives about 0.982. A value between 0 and 1 looks like a probability, but whether it deserves that reading depends on how the model was trained and checked; §4 returns to outputs that are meant as probabilities.</Prose>

<H3>{"The same calculation has a geometric meaning"}</H3>
<Prose>{"With two inputs, every possible input "}<Math>{"(x_1,x_2)"}</Math>{" is a point in a plane, and the neuron gives each point a score. Which points score exactly 0? Those with "}<Math>{"1.5x_1-2x_2-1=0"}</Math>{", which rearranges to "}<Math>{"x_2=0.75x_1-0.5"}</Math>{": a straight line. The picture shades the plane by the sign of the score."}</Prose>
<ScoreLevelFigure />
<Prose>{"The white line is the neuron's "}<strong>{"decision boundary"}</strong>{": points on the amber side score positive and get output 1; points on the blue side score 0 or less and get output 0. The dashed lines are two other score levels, −4 and 4. They run parallel to the boundary because along any of them the two contributions trade off exactly and the score stays fixed. The gold point is the drawn neuron's input (2, −1), on the score-4 line."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"Why does the arrow "}<Math>{"w"}</Math>{" cross the lines at a right angle? Moving from one point to another changes the score by "}<Math>{"w^\\top(\\text{step})"}</Math>{". A step along a level line leaves the score unchanged, so that step has zero dot product with "}<Math>{"w"}</Math>{": the weight vector is perpendicular to the boundary and points toward increasing scores. A step of length 1 in the direction of "}<Math>{"w"}</Math>{" raises the score by the length "}<Math>{"\\|w\\|_2"}</Math>{". So to convert a score into a distance, divide by that length. For nonzero "}<Math>{"w"}</Math>{", the signed Euclidean distance to the boundary is"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"d(x)=\\frac{w^\\top x+b}{\\|w\\|_2}."}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"Our weight vector has length "}<Math>{"\\sqrt{1.5^2+(-2)^2}=2.5"}</Math>{", so the point scoring 4 lies 4 / 2.5 = 1.6 input-coordinate units from the line, along the dashed perpendicular. The score is "}<strong>{"not"}</strong>{" the distance in general. Multiplying every weight and the bias by 2 changes the score to 8 but keeps the same line and the same distance, because the length doubles too. The hard decision is unchanged, while sigmoid moves closer to 1: boundary geometry and output confidence are different properties."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"If every weight is 0, the score is the bias at every point: there is no line and no length to divide by. With more than two inputs the same equation describes a "}<strong>{"hyperplane"}</strong>{", a flat boundary with one fewer dimension than the input space. Investigation A's nine-pixel template defines one such hyperplane in a nine-dimensional space of images."}</Prose>
<Prose><strong>{"Investigation B — move the point, then rescale."}</strong>{" The plane below is the same neuron. The three bars beside the plane are the score's parts, w₁x₁, w₂x₂ and the bias, drawn from a shared zero (amber positive, blue negative); the white square on the line is the foot of the shortest path from the point to the boundary, whose length is the distance. Raise or lower the point with its "}<Math>{"x_2"}</Math>{" slider and watch it cross the line at "}<Math>{"x_2=1"}</Math>{", where it sits exactly on the boundary and the strict rule outputs 0. Then change the common scale "}<Math>{"c"}</Math>{", which multiplies both weights and the bias: the line stays put and the distance stays the same, while the score and the sigmoid output change."}</Prose>
<GeometryLab />

<H2>{"2. A perceptron learns a boundary from mistakes"}</H2>
<Prose>{"So far we chose the weights by thinking about the task. For a digit with 64 pixels and ten classes, nobody wants to design every template by hand. Can a rule find weights from labelled examples? The "}<strong>{"perceptron"}</strong>{" is the classic first answer: the neuron of §1 with a hard threshold, plus an update that corrects the weights each time it gets an example wrong. It is worth learning because you can watch every correction. Frank Rosenblatt's perceptron of the late 1950s is an early landmark of machine learning; what we need from it here is the mechanism, how a mistake moves a boundary, rather than its history."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"The perceptron writes labels as "}<Math>{"y\\in\\{-1,+1\\}"}</Math>{" instead of 0 and 1. The reason is a convenient test: a positive example should score positive and a negative example should score negative, so in both cases the prediction is right exactly when the product "}<Math>{"yz"}</Math>{" is positive. This product is the example's "}<strong>{"margin"}</strong>{". For label +1 and score 3 the margin is 3; for label −1 and score 3 it is −3, a mistake; a score of exactly 0 gives margin 0, a tie. The perceptron updates whenever the margin is "}<Math>{"\\le 0"}</Math>{", so ties count as mistakes during training."}</Prose>
<H3>Follow one correction before the rule</H3>
<Prose>{"Take the point "}<Math>{"x=(1,2)"}</Math>{" with label +1, and current weights "}<Math>{"w=(-1,0)"}</Math>{", bias 0. Its score is "}<Math>{"(-1)(1)+0(2)+0=-1"}</Math>{": the wrong sign, so the margin is −1, a mistake. The figure shows a correction that fixes it: add half of the input, with a 1 appended for the bias, to the parameters."}</Prose>
<MistakeCorrectionFigure />
<Prose>{"Read the columns. "}<Math>{"w_1"}</Math>{" multiplies "}<Math>{"x_1=1"}</Math>{", so raising it by 0.5 raises the score by 0.5. "}<Math>{"w_2"}</Math>{" multiplies "}<Math>{"x_2=2"}</Math>{", so raising it by 1 raises the score by 2. The bias rises by 0.5, and so does every score. Together the score rises by 3, from −1 to 2, and the example is now correct. Changing each weight in proportion to its own input is what makes the correction effective: inputs with larger values have more leverage on the score."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"Written generally, with step size "}<Math>{"\\eta>0"}</Math>{" (the “half” above), a mistaken example updates"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"w\\leftarrow w+\\eta yx,\\qquad b\\leftarrow b+\\eta y."}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"The label's sign chooses the direction: a mistaken +1 example has its input added, a mistaken −1 example has it subtracted. On that same example the signed score rises by "}<Math>{"\\eta(\\|x\\|^2+1)"}</Math>{", always a positive amount; in our case 0.5 × (1 + 4 + 1) = 3. Other examples can improve or worsen, because their inputs receive the same change with different products. "}<a href="https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs4780/2022sp/notes/LectureNotes06.html">{"Cornell's perceptron notes"}</a>{" derive this rule and its guarantee."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"Code usually hides the bias inside the weights by appending a constant input 1 to every example. Its weight then "}<em>{"is"}</em>{" the bias: it contributes an offset even when every measured input is zero, and the update above adds "}<Math>{"\\eta y\\cdot 1"}</Math>{" to it automatically. One dot product and one update handle both the slopes and the offset. The augmented vector for "}<Math>{"(1,2)"}</Math>{" is "}<Math>{"(1,2,1)"}</Math>{", the three columns in the figure."}</Prose>
<H3>Train on complete tasks</H3>
<Prose>{"Training repeats the correction over a dataset: present each example in a fixed order, update on each mistake, and call one full pass over the data an "}<strong>{"epoch"}</strong>{". Stop after an epoch with no updates. Consider AND: an alarm should trigger only if "}<strong>{"both"}</strong>{" binary indicators are 1. Four rows define the complete task; the last column defines a second task for comparison."}</Prose>
<div className="perceptron-table" role="region" tabIndex={0} aria-label="AND and XOR labels for the four corners"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">{"Inputs"}</th><th scope="col">{"AND label"}</th><th scope="col">{"XOR label"}</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">{"(0, 0)"}</th><td>{"−1"}</td><td>{"−1"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"(0, 1)"}</th><td>{"−1"}</td><td>{"+1"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"(1, 0)"}</th><td>{"−1"}</td><td>{"+1"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"(1, 1)"}</th><td>{"+1"}</td><td>{"−1"}</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<Prose>{"XOR means exactly one indicator is 1. The two tasks look equally small, but they behave completely differently."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"Start at zero weights with step size 1. The first example (0, 0) scores 0, a tie, so it updates: the label is −1, so the bias drops to −1. Corners 01 and 10 now score −1 with label −1: margin +1, no change. Corner 11 scores −1 with label +1: a mistake, so add (1, 1, 1) and reach "}<Math>{"(w_1,w_2,b)=(1,1,0)"}</Math>{" at the end of epoch 1. Continue this yourself below."}</Prose>
<Prose><strong>Investigation C — step through training.</strong> The plane shows the four corners with their labels (amber +1, blue −1) and the current boundary. It opens where the walk-through above stopped, at the end of AND's epoch 1 with (1, 1, 0); Back replays that epoch. Present the next corner and follow the score, the margin, the update and the moving line; the white ring marks the corner just presented. Then run AND to the end. Switching to OR or XOR starts that task from zero weights; run each to the end.</Prose>
<PerceptronTraceLab />
<Prose>{"Here is the same procedure as a complete NumPy program. Install NumPy if your environment does not have it: "}<Code>{"python -m pip install numpy"}</Code>{". It appends the constant 1 to each row so the bias is simply the last coefficient, and prints each epoch's coefficients, update count and final predictions."}</Prose>
<RunnableExample example={perceptronExamples[0]} />
<Prose>{"The tie policy has two parts that are deliberately different: at prediction time, score 0 predicts −1; during training, a zero margin updates for either label."}</Prose>
<Prose>The same algorithm is available in scikit-learn as <Code>Perceptron</Code>. Matching it to the loop above means matching every choice: the same four rows in the same order (<Code>shuffle=False</Code>), step size 1 (<Code>eta0=1</Code>), no penalty, and twelve passes with early stopping disabled (<Code>tol=None</Code>). Under those settings it reaches the same AND coefficients (3, 2, −4) and the same zero-coefficient XOR cycle. Our loop stops after a no-update pass, whereas the library runs all twelve; running the stationary AND model for the remaining passes changes nothing. <a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.linear_model.Perceptron.html">The Perceptron API</a> documents these settings; the complete comparison program at the end of §7 runs this check.</Prose>
<Prose>{"On AND, the run ends with "}<Math>{"(w_1,w_2,b)=(3,2,-4)"}</Math>{": the four scores are −4, −2, −1 and 1, all with the correct sign. Epoch 8 makes one update and epoch 9 none, so training stops. Predictions were already correct after epoch 3, yet updates continued because some corners sat exactly on the line with margin 0. An epoch's update count also includes mistakes made by intermediate weights; it is not the number of errors in the final model."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"On XOR, every one of the 12 epochs makes four updates and returns the coefficients to 0, as Investigation C shows. The final rule predicts −1 for every row: two final errors, not four. More epochs cannot fix this, and the next subsection shows why."}</Prose>
<H3>{"Why XOR is impossible for one boundary"}</H3>
<Prose>{"Suppose some line did separate XOR. For "}<Math>{"(1,0)"}</Math>{" and "}<Math>{"(0,1)"}</Math>{" to be positive, we need"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"w_1+b>0,\\qquad w_2+b>0."}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"Adding gives "}<Math>{"w_1+w_2+2 b>0"}</Math>{". But the two negative examples require "}<Math>{"b\\le 0"}</Math>{" (from (0, 0)) and "}<Math>{"w_1+w_2+b\\le 0"}</Math>{" (from (1, 1)), which imply "}<Math>{"w_1+w_2+2 b\\le b\\le 0"}</Math>{". The demands contradict one another, so no weights exist. Geometrically, the positive corners sit on one diagonal of the square and the negative corners on the other; any straight cut that keeps both positive corners together also captures a negative one."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"The perceptron convergence theorem covers the opposite situation: when some strict separating boundary exists and inputs are bounded, the number of updates is finite. It says nothing about XOR, noisy labels or contradictory duplicate examples, and it does not promise the widest possible margin."}</Prose>
<Prose><strong>Deeper branch — why a finite update bound holds.</strong> Imagine a direction that would classify every training row correctly. Each mistaken-row update makes some minimum progress toward that direction. At the same time, the weight vector’s overall length cannot grow fast enough to permit that progress forever. The proof compares these two growth rates; the algorithm never needs to know the successful direction.</Prose>
<Prose>{"Absorb the bias into augmented inputs, suppose their lengths are at most "}<Math>{"R"}</Math>{", and suppose a unit vector "}<Math>{"u"}</Math>{" separates them with "}<Math>{"y_i u^\\top x_i\\ge\\gamma>0"}</Math>{". With step size 1 and zero initialization, after "}<Math>{"M"}</Math>{" updates, "}<Math>{"w^\\top u\\ge M\\gamma"}</Math>{", whereas "}<Math>{"\\|w\\|^2\\le MR^2"}</Math>{". The latter follows from expanding one update, "}<Math>{"\\|w+yx\\|^2=\\|w\\|^2+2y\\,w^\\top x+\\|x\\|^2"}</Math>{": the middle (cross) term is "}<Math>{"\\le 0"}</Math>{" because updates happen only on mistakes, where "}<Math>{"y\\,w^\\top x\\le 0"}</Math>{", so each update adds at most "}<Math>{"R^2"}</Math>{". Cauchy–Schwarz gives "}<Math>{"M\\gamma\\le\\sqrt M R"}</Math>{", hence "}<Math>{"M\\le(R/\\gamma)^2"}</Math>{". This counts updates, not epochs."}</Prose>
<Prose>In that proof, the projection toward the successful unit direction grows at least like M, while the vector length grows at most like √M. A projection cannot exceed the vector’s length. Eventually those demands conflict, so updates must stop. A small margin γ permits a much larger bound: examples nearly touching a separating boundary are harder to separate by these corrections.</Prose>

<H2>{"3. Hidden neurons change what the model can express"}</H2>
<Prose>{"One neuron draws one straight boundary, and XOR needs something else. The repair is to let earlier neurons compute new features, and let a final neuron draw its line on those features instead of the raw inputs. Neurons between the input and the final output form a "}<strong>{"hidden layer"}</strong>{". “Hidden” only means they are neither input nor final output; their values are ordinary numbers you can print."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"Which features would help? Notice that XOR only depends on how many inputs are on. Let "}<Math>{"s=x_1+x_2"}</Math>{". For a network that outputs a number we compare directly, it is simpler to use targets 0 and 1 instead of §2's −1 and +1: XOR wants 0 at "}<Math>{"s=0"}</Math>{", 1 at "}<Math>{"s=1"}</Math>{" and 0 at "}<Math>{"s=2"}</Math>{": up, then down again. A ReLU ramp only goes up; the figure shows how a second, delayed ramp bends the total back down."}</Prose>
<XorRampFigure />
<Prose>{"Read the curves at the three dots. At "}<Math>{"s=0"}</Math>{" both ramps are 0. At "}<Math>{"s=1"}</Math>{" the first ramp has risen to 1 and the second has not started, so the total is 1. At "}<Math>{"s=2"}</Math>{" the first ramp is 2 and the second ramp is 1, counted −2 times, so the total returns to 0. As two ReLU hidden neurons and one output neuron:"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"h_1=\\max(0,s),\\qquad h_2=\\max(0,s-1),\\qquad q=h_1-2 h_2."}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"Each hidden neuron is an ordinary neuron from §1: both have weights (1, 1) on the two inputs, with biases 0 and −1, followed by ReLU. The output neuron has weights 1 and −2 on the hidden values, bias 0 and no activation. Here is the whole network, drawn the same way as §1's neuron."}</Prose>
<XorNetworkLab />
<Prose>{"The cyan circles hold the two inputs; they are data, so they have no weights or activation. Each violet circle "}<Math>{"h_1"}</Math>{", "}<Math>{"h_2"}</Math>{" stands for a complete §1 neuron: its two incoming arrows each deliver an input times weight 1, its bias is written beside it, and the label above it names its activation. Their outputs travel on to the final neuron "}<Math>{"q"}</Math>{", which applies no activation and passes its score on unchanged. Choose a corner: the three lines under the drawing trace exactly what each circle computes, and every corner matches XOR's target."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"The network has two learned layers, the two hidden neurons side by side and then the output neuron: six connection weights and three biases. Calling the input circles an “input layer” does not add a parameterized layer, and the same six weights serve every example. Written as matrices with one row per receiving neuron, "}<Math>{"W_1=\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\1&1\\end{bmatrix}"}</Math>{", "}<Math>{"b_1=(0,-1)"}</Math>{", "}<Math>{"W_2=[1,\\,-2]"}</Math>{", "}<Math>{"b_2=0"}</Math>{": row 1 of "}<Math>{"W_1"}</Math>{" holds the two wires into "}<Math>{"h_1"}</Math>{". The batch rule at the end of this section stores weights in exactly this layout."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"The first hidden neuron is a "}<strong>{"ramp"}</strong>{", not a Boolean OR gate: at (1, 1) it outputs 2. The second ramp starts one unit later, and subtracting twice the delayed ramp cancels the both-on case. Now switch the hidden activation to identity, which passes each hidden score on unchanged. At (1, 1) both choices give 0, so one example alone cannot tell you whether the hidden activations matter; at (0, 0) identity gives 2, and the network is no longer XOR. The subsection after next explains why."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"There is a second way to see why the ReLU network works, which connects directly to §2. Treat the two hidden values as new coordinates and plot where each corner lands."}</Prose>
<HiddenSpaceFigure />
<Prose>{"Corners 01 and 10 both land at "}<Math>{"(h_1,h_2)=(1,0)"}</Math>{"; 00 stays at (0, 0); 11 moves to (2, 1). In this new plane one straight line separates the target-1 point from the others. The figure draws it where "}<Math>{"q=h_1-2h_2"}</Math>{" equals 0.5, halfway between the targets 0 and 1, so “q above 0.5” reads the exact output as a yes/no answer. The hidden layer did not solve XOR by itself: it moved the examples so that the output neuron's single line is enough."}</Prose>
<Prose><strong>{"Investigation D — repair the whole truth table."}</strong>{" Call the second output weight "}<Math>{"v"}</Math>{" (the working network has "}<Math>{"v=-2"}</Math>{"), and call each row's output minus its target its "}<strong>{"residual"}</strong>{": 0 when the row is right. The table below starts from "}<Math>{"v=-1"}</Math>{", where the both-on row is wrong. Move "}<Math>{"v"}</Math>{" while watching all four residuals. Then move the second hidden bias from −1 to −0.5 and look for a "}<Math>{"v"}</Math>{" that repairs every row: a change that repairs one corner can damage another."}</Prose>
<XorLab />
<Prose>{"We chose these weights by reasoning, as in §1. That shows the network "}<em>{"can"}</em>{" express XOR; finding such weights from examples is the separate training problem §5 turns to."}</Prose>
<H3>{"Why the bend is essential"}</H3>
<Prose>{"A "}<strong>{"bend"}</strong>{" is ReLU's corner at score 0: below it the output is flat at 0, above it the output follows the score. Remove the two ReLUs, as the identity setting above did, and keep everything else: "}<Math>{"h_1=s"}</Math>{", "}<Math>{"h_2=s-1"}</Math>{", so "}<Math>{"q=s-2(s-1)=2-s"}</Math>{". That is a single straight function of "}<Math>{"s"}</Math>{", giving 2, 1, 0 at the three counts: wrong at (0, 0) and no longer a tent. Without the bend, the two hidden neurons collapse into one line. This holds in general. In column-vector notation, where "}<Math>{"W_1x"}</Math>{" multiplies a weight matrix by one input column, two affine layers compose to"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"W_2(W_1 x+b_1)+b_2=(W_2 W_1)x+(W_2 b_1+b_2)."}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"That is another single affine map. Stacking more affine layers changes the parameterization but cannot create a bend. The argument concerns a chain containing only affine operations; an architecture can contain other nonlinear operations, such as normalization or attention, even without a named activation."}</Prose>
<H3>Many examples and many neurons at once</H3>
<Prose>{"Code processes a batch of examples together, and it writes each example as a "}<strong>{"row"}</strong>{". A batch "}<Math>{"X"}</Math>{" of "}<Math>{"N"}</Math>{" examples with "}<Math>{"d"}</Math>{" inputs each has shape "}<Math>{"(N,d)"}</Math>{". A layer of "}<Math>{"m"}</Math>{" neurons keeps one weight row per neuron, the wires into that neuron, as a matrix "}<Math>{"W"}</Math>{" of shape "}<Math>{"(m,d)"}</Math>{", and one bias per neuron. The figure sends two examples through a layer of two neurons; its first example and first neuron are §1's drawn neuron, now with ReLU as the activation instead of the step rule, so the outputs can be any non-negative number."}</Prose>
<BatchNeuronCorrespondence />
<Prose>{"Each score is one example's row dotted with one neuron's weight row, plus that neuron's bias. Example 1, neuron 1: 2 × 1.5 + (−1) × (−2) − 1 = 4, exactly §1's score. Example 1, neuron 2: 2 × (−1) + (−1) × 1 + 0.5 = −2.5. The second example uses the same two weight rows and biases. In matrix form the whole table is"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"Z=XW^\\top+b,\\qquad A=\\phi(Z),"}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"with shape "}<Math>{"(N,m)"}</Math>{". Entry "}<Math>{"Z_{nj}"}</Math>{" is neuron "}<Math>{"j"}</Math>{"'s score for example "}<Math>{"n"}</Math>{". The transpose puts each neuron's weights in a column so that matrix multiplication forms those dot products. The same bias vector is added to every example row, and the activation acts on each entry separately. "}<a href="https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.14/generated/torch.nn.Linear.html">{"PyTorch Linear"}</a>{" stores weights in this output-by-input orientation."}</Prose>
<Prose>Read a batch result in two directions: a row answers every neuron's question for one example; a column answers one neuron's question for every example. A batch of 5 images with 64 inputs through 32 hidden neurons becomes a 5 × 32 activation matrix. Adding examples adds rows without adding learned parameters, and an arrow in a network diagram represents a weight, not an example. Practice 4 carries this into a larger shape.</Prose>

<H2>{"4. Activation functions shape values and sensitivities"}</H2>
<Prose>{"The perceptron's update in §2 only reacted to outright mistakes, one neuron at a time. A multi-layer network needs a finer signal: for every weight, "}<em>{"if I nudge it slightly, does the result get better or worse, and by how much?"}</em>{" The next lesson computes that for a whole network. The raw material is simple: how much does each activation's output move when its score moves a little? Start with the three activations from §1, drawn as curves."}</Prose>
<SlopeCurveFigure />
<Prose>{"Read each curve where it is marked. The step is flat on both sides of 0, so a small change in score moves its output not at all, except right at 0, where the output jumps from 0 to 1. ReLU is flat for negative scores and rises one-for-one for positive ones; its tangent lies on the curve itself. Sigmoid is steepest at 0 and nearly flat at 5. To put numbers on that steepness, nudge each marked score by 0.01 and measure how far the output moves. The table gives the exact values behind the dots."}</Prose>
<NudgeSlopeFigure />
<Prose>{"For the smooth rows, the change divided by the nudge and the exact slope nearly agree (0.249998 and 0.25; 0.006615 and 0.006648): the exact slope is what that ratio approaches as the nudge shrinks. It is the "}<strong>{"derivative"}</strong>{" "}<Math>{"\\phi'(z)"}</Math>{", the local slope: near the current score, a small change "}<Math>{"\\Delta z"}</Math>{" produces approximately "}<Math>{"\\phi'(z)\\Delta z"}</Math>{". The hard threshold's slope is zero everywhere except at the jump, where it is undefined. So in a network of hard-threshold neurons, a small weight change usually changes nothing downstream, and when it does, an output flips abruptly. The table's last row starts 0.005 below the jump: its exact slope there is still 0, but the 0.01 nudge crosses the jump at "}<Math>{"z=0"}</Math>{" and flips the output from 0 to 1, a ratio of 100 that describes the jump, not the flat function on either side. That combination of silence and sudden flips gives training no graded signal to follow, which is why trainable networks use activations like these instead:"}</Prose>
<div className="perceptron-table" role="region" tabIndex={0} aria-label="Activation functions, their output rules and local slopes"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">{"Function"}</th><th scope="col">{"Output rule"}</th><th scope="col">{"Local slope"}</th><th scope="col">{"What to notice"}</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">{"Sigmoid"}</th><td><Math>{"\\sigma(z)=1/(1+e^{-z})"}</Math></td><td><Math>{"\\sigma(z)(1-\\sigma(z))"}</Math></td><td>{"Bounded between 0 and 1; flat tails"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"Tanh"}</th><td><Math>{"\\tanh(z)"}</Math></td><td><Math>{"1-\\tanh^2(z)"}</Math></td><td>{"Bounded between −1 and 1; centered at 0"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"ReLU"}</th><td><Math>{"\\max(0,z)"}</Math></td><td>{"0 for "}<Math>{"z<0"}</Math>{", 1 for "}<Math>{"z>0"}</Math></td><td>{"Removes negative scores, keeps positive scores"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"Leaky ReLU"}</th><td><Math>{"z"}</Math>{" if "}<Math>{"z\\ge 0"}</Math>{", else "}<Math>{"0.1z"}</Math></td><td>{"1 for "}<Math>{"z>0"}</Math>{", 0.1 for "}<Math>{"z<0"}</Math></td><td>{"Like ReLU, but negative scores keep a small slope (more in §7)"}</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<Prose>{"Tanh is a rescaled sigmoid centred at 0: "}<Math>{"\\tanh(z)=2\\sigma(2z)-1"}</Math>{". At zero, ReLU has a corner and no ordinary derivative; a framework chooses a convention there, and PyTorch uses 0. The forward function remains well-defined."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"At "}<Math>{"z=-2"}</Math>{", sigmoid is about 0.1192, tanh −0.9640 and ReLU 0. At "}<Math>{"z=2"}</Math>{", their outputs are 0.8808, 0.9640 and 2. Sigmoid's maximum slope is 0.25 at 0; tanh's is 1 at 0. A sigmoid at 5 still has slope about 0.00665: small, not zero. This is what “flat tails” means: once the score is far from 0, nudging it barely moves the output."}</Prose>
<H3>{"Slopes belong to a whole computation"}</H3>
<Prose>{"A neuron's output also responds to its input through the weight. Take §1's neuron with a single input: "}<Math>{"a=\\phi(wx+b)"}</Math>{". A small change in "}<Math>{"x"}</Math>{" first changes the score by "}<Math>{"w"}</Math>{" times that amount, and the activation then scales it by its slope:"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"\\frac{da}{dx}=\\phi'(z)w,\\qquad \\frac{da}{dw}=\\phi'(z)x."}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"The second formula answers this section's opening question about nudging a weight: its effect is the activation slope times the input it multiplies. An input of 0 makes its weight's nudge irrelevant, just as blank pixels did in Investigation A. The next lesson chains exactly these products through a network."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"So a sigmoid's 0.25 maximum slope does not mean every layer shrinks every signal by 0.25: if "}<Math>{"w=4"}</Math>{" and "}<Math>{"z=0"}</Math>{", the product is 1. Conversely, a positive ReLU with "}<Math>{"w=0.5"}</Math>{" transmits a factor 0.5 despite its activation slope being 1. Across many layers these factors multiply, so they can shrink or grow; in vector layers the weight matrices and their directions matter too."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"A ReLU whose score is negative for every example in the current batch has slope 0 there, so it passes no signal back for those examples. It may be active on another batch. A unit that stays negative on the whole training distribution can be hard to recover through that path, although momentum, another path or changes in earlier features can change the situation. Diagnose actual activations and gradients rather than trusting a universal “too many zeros” percentage."}</Prose>
<Prose><strong>{"Investigation E — output, slope and sensitivity."}</strong>{" Choose an activation, then move the score "}<Math>{"z"}</Math>{" and the incoming weight "}<Math>{"w"}</Math>{". The left plot marks the output at the dashed operating point, the right plot its local slope, and the sentence underneath multiplies "}<Math>{"w"}</Math>{" by that slope. Changing w here keeps z fixed, as if the bias adjusted to compensate, so the sentence isolates the product w·φ′(z). Positive ReLU and leaky ReLU agree; move to a negative score to expose their difference. A large output is not the same thing as a large derivative."}</Prose>
<ActivationLab />
<section id="perceptron-code-route">
<H3>Implement the neuron, then match the library</H3>
<Prose>You can now write the three activations you have met as code. The function <Code>activation(name, z)</Code> below returns each value and its local slope for a whole array of scores. The sigmoid branch computes <Code>exp(-abs(z))</Code> so a very negative score never needs the overflowing exponential e<sup>−z</sup>; the ReLU slope uses PyTorch's convention 0 at z = 0. The program then checks §3's batch rule against PyTorch: <Code>nn.Linear</Code> stores its weight as (outputs, inputs), so copying the same W and b gives exactly <Code>X @ W.T + b</Code>. Finally it computes Investigation E's input sensitivity for a sigmoid neuron at z = 0 with incoming weights 0, 0.5 and 4.</Prose>
<CodeBlock language="python" filename="neuron_core.py" title="Three activations, the nn.Linear layout and input sensitivity">{neuronCoreProgram}</CodeBlock>
<Prose>Run it with NumPy and PyTorch installed (<Code>python neuron_core.py</Code>). Output recorded in the lesson's tested environment:</Prose>
<CodeBlock language="text" kind="output" filename="neuron_core-output.txt" title="neuron_core.py output">{neuronCoreOutput}</CodeBlock>
<Prose>Read the output against the earlier sections. The sigmoid, tanh and ReLU values at −2, 0 and 2 are the numbers above, and the slopes peak at 0.25 and 1 at z = 0. The two layer results reproduce §3's batch figure by two independent routes. The last three lines are da/dx = σ′(0)·w: local slope 0.25 becomes sensitivities 0, 0.125 and 1, so an activation's maximum slope alone does not fix how much signal a neuron passes. The full comparison of all nine activations against their library modules is at the end of §7, after those functions are defined.</Prose>
</section>
<H3>{"Output interpretation is a separate choice"}</H3>
<Prose>{"A digit classifier needs ten outputs, one per class, and they compete: an image should not be both a confident 3 and a confident 8. The final layer therefore produces one unrestricted score per class, called a "}<strong>{"logit"}</strong>{", and a separate step turns the logits into shares that are positive and sum to 1. That step is "}<strong>{"softmax"}</strong>{": exponentiate each score, which makes every value positive and turns a score difference of 1 into a factor of "}<Math>{"e\\approx 2.72"}</Math>{", then divide each exponential by their total. The figure applies it to two sets of three scores."}</Prose>
<SoftmaxCompetitionFigure />
<Prose>{"In the first strip the logits are (1, 2, 3): the exponentials are about 2.72, 7.39 and 20.09, the total is 30.19, and the shares are approximately (0.0900, 0.2447, 0.6652). In the second strip only the third score changed, from 3 to 4, so its exponential grew from 20.09 to 54.60. The total grew with it, and the first two shares shrank even though their own scores did not move; their ratio stays "}<Math>{"e^1:e^2\\approx 1:2.72"}</Math>{". Three independent sigmoids would have left the first two outputs unchanged. As a formula:"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"p_k=\\frac{e^{z_k-c}}{\\sum_j e^{z_j-c}},\\qquad c=\\max_j z_j."}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"The formula also subtracts the largest score "}<Math>{"c"}</Math>{" first. That divides every exponential by the same positive factor "}<Math>{"e^c"}</Math>{", which cancels between numerator and denominator, so the shares are unchanged; it only keeps the exponentials from overflowing. Adding 1000 to every logit therefore preserves the shares when the stable formula is used. Very negative differences can still underflow to 0."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"Softmax combines coordinates, so it is not a pointwise hidden activation. A binary probability output often uses a single sigmoid; independent yes/no labels, such as tags that can co-occur, use separate sigmoids; mutually exclusive classes commonly use softmax; a real-valued regression target may need an unrestricted output. Choose the output from the target and loss, not from the hidden activation. The classifier below passes raw logits to cross-entropy, a library loss that performs the stable log-probability calculation itself. Whether the resulting shares are trustworthy probabilities is a separate calibration question for the evaluation lessons."}</Prose>

<H2>{"5. Train a small network on real handwriting"}</H2>
<Prose>{"Now return to the opening problem with everything assembled: 64 pixel inputs, a hidden layer of §1 neurons, and ten logits turned into shares by softmax. The previous Classical ML capstone separated fitting, model choice and final reporting. Keep that discipline here: a neural model is another candidate function class, judged by the same protocol."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"Our offline file contains 400 actual digit images, 40 per class, from E. Alpaydin and C. Kaynak's "}<strong>{"UCI Optical Recognition of Handwritten Digits"}</strong>{" dataset. Each 8×8 image has 64 integer values from 0 to 16. These are counts of active pixels in original 4×4 blocks, not arbitrary intensity values invented for a plot. The file is a balanced selection from scikit-learn's 1797-row copy of the historical UCI test partition, and we make a fresh instructional train/validation split within it. "}<a href="https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/80/optical+recognition+of+handwritten+">{"Dataset source and CC BY 4.0 attribution"}</a>{", "}<a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.datasets.load_digits.html">{"loader description"}</a>{". Choose a digit to see the 64 numbers a network receives for it."}</Prose>
<DigitFigure />
<Prose>{"Each of the 32 hidden neurons is a §1 template over all 64 pixels, but nobody designs it: training chooses its weights. The question here is narrow: "}<strong>{"under the same small training protocol, how much does hidden activation choice change training loss and validation mistakes?"}</strong>{" We predeclare six activations and three initialization "}<strong>{"seeds"}</strong>{", the starting values of the random number generator that picks initial weights. For each seed, resetting before model construction gives every activation the same initial affine parameters. The data split, width, optimizer and number of updates stay fixed."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"To train, the model needs one number saying how wrong it currently is: a "}<strong>{"loss"}</strong>{". Here it is mean cross-entropy: for each image, take the softmax share assigned to its actual digit, compute the negative natural logarithm, and average over images. A correct-label probability of 0.5 contributes about 0.693; a probability of 0.9 contributes about 0.105; a probability near 0 contributes a very large amount. The loss therefore rewards confident correct predictions and punishes confident wrong ones."}</Prose>
<Prose>Why also inspect loss when we already count correct digits? If the true class wins with probability 0.51 or 0.99, both cases count as correct, but they contribute about 0.673 and 0.010 to cross-entropy. The loss sees that difference in assigned probability; a correct-count metric discards it. It also changes smoothly when a weight is nudged, which is exactly the graded signal §4 said training needs. The experiment measures both because they answer different questions.</Prose>
<Prose>{"Training repeats one step many times. Compare it with §2: the perceptron computed a score, checked a margin, and nudged weights in a direction that fixed one example. Here every weight is nudged at once, in the direction that lowers the loss over all training images. The figure lays out one step."}</Prose>
<TrainingLoopStructure />
<Prose>{"Steps 1–5 run forward through the network you have already met. Step 6 produces the "}<strong>{"gradient"}</strong>{": for each parameter, how the loss changes when that parameter is nudged, which is §4's slopes combined through the whole network. The next lesson opens that differentiation step. Step 7 uses an "}<strong>{"optimizer"}</strong>{", the rule that turns gradients into parameter changes; the simplest subtracts a small multiple of each gradient. Adam, used here, also keeps running averages to adapt each step size; you do not need its internal equations to follow this experiment. Before the next step, the old gradients are cleared so each step's gradients come from its own forward pass. The Loss Functions lesson explains the objective in detail."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"Save the following program as "}<Code>{"compare_activations.py"}</Code>{" and keep the accompanying download "}<Code>{"digits-400.csv"}</Code>{" in the same directory. No data download is required when it runs. The author calculation used Python 3.12.14, NumPy 2.3.5, scikit-learn 1.9.1 and PyTorch 2.14.0+cpu. For a fresh CPU environment:"}</Prose>
<CodeBlock language="text">{"python -m pip install numpy==2.3.5 scikit-learn==1.9.1\npython -m pip install torch==2.14.0 --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu\npython compare_activations.py"}</CodeBlock>
<RunnableExample example={perceptronExamples[1]} />
<Prose>{"Dividing by 16 uses the known measurement range, not a statistic fitted using validation examples. The matrices have shapes 280×64 → 280×32 → 280×10 during training, the §3 batch rule with "}<Math>{"N=280"}</Math>{". Labels have shape 280 and integer class IDs 0–9. The last layer has no sigmoid or softmax because cross-entropy expects logits."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"The default linear-layer initialization is held fixed across activation comparisons, rather than tailored to each one; initialization is a later topic. These activation modules contain no learned parameters, although some other activation families, such as PReLU, do."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"The executed author calculation, using the same data and update loop, produced:"}</Prose>
<div className="perceptron-table" role="region" tabIndex={0} aria-label="Measured training loss and validation counts for six activations and three seeds"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">{"Activation"}</th><th scope="col">{"Training loss, seeds 1 / 2 / 3"}</th><th scope="col">{"Validation correct, seeds 1 / 2 / 3"}</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">{"Sigmoid"}</th><td>{".017062 / .015584 / .016383"}</td><td>{"118 / 118 / 117"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"Tanh"}</th><td>{".003791 / .003635 / .003606"}</td><td>{"117 / 118 / 117"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"ReLU"}</th><td>{".002392 / .003283 / .002958"}</td><td>{"118 / 118 / 116"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"Leaky ReLU, slope .1"}</th><td>{".002210 / .001647 / .002250"}</td><td>{"118 / 118 / 116"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"GELU, exact"}</th><td>{".001848 / .002123 / .002102"}</td><td>{"118 / 118 / 117"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"SiLU"}</th><td>{".001847 / .002045 / .001948"}</td><td>{"118 / 117 / 117"}</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<Prose>{"Training cross-entropy is a mean in natural-log units per example; smaller means a higher geometric mean of the probabilities assigned to true training labels. Validation counts have denominator 120, so a one-case difference is 0.833 percentage points. Choose a seed below to see how each loss fell over the run and where each validation count sits; choose a second activation to list the validation images either network got wrong."}</Prose>
<DigitComparisonFigure />
<Prose>{"Sigmoid fits this shallow, scaled task well. Lower training loss does not consistently yield fewer validation errors, and several comparisons are exact ties in correct count. Read the list of wrong images too: are two networks wrong on the same examples? A difference of one in the total could hide several repairs and new mistakes, just as in the capstone."}</Prose>
<Prose><strong>{"What this experiment can and cannot show."}</strong>{" It compares six activations under one fixed protocol, with matched starting weights, on 400 images from one historical partition. It does not tune each activation to its own best settings, reproduce the original writer-separated benchmark, or measure transfer to unseen writers. Three seeds support neither a universal ranking nor a claim that the functions are equivalent, and changing width, learning rate, depth, split or number of updates makes a new experiment. The validation results have now been used for comparison, so a final claim would need a fresh test set. The displayed programs were replayed in the recorded CPU environment, and their outputs are shown above; a fresh installation was not separately tested."}</Prose>

<H2>{"6. Choose and diagnose with the mechanism in mind"}</H2>
<Prose>{"With the mechanism and one controlled experiment in hand, choosing an activation starts from the role of the layer and the surrounding architecture. ReLU is a simple hidden-layer baseline. Smooth GELU or SiLU variants are worth comparing when the architecture and optimization protocol motivate them. Sigmoid remains useful for probabilities and bounded gates; tanh for centered bounded transformations. A function's popularity does not replace a validation experiment."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"Before changing the activation, check the evidence that would distinguish causes:"}</Prose>
<div className="perceptron-table" role="region" tabIndex={0} aria-label="Observations, what to inspect, and why an activation swap may not fix them"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">{"Observation"}</th><th scope="col">{"What to inspect"}</th><th scope="col">{"Why an activation swap may not fix it"}</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">{"Loss never changes"}</th><td>{"Parameter registration, gradients, optimizer step, learning rate"}</td><td>{"A disconnected parameter cannot learn through any activation"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"Many zeros after ReLU"}</th><td>{"Preactivations by example and layer, gradient paths"}</td><td>{"See §4: zero on one batch is not permanent inactivity"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"Tiny gradients"}</th><td>{"Local slopes, weight scales, depth and loss scaling"}</td><td>{"Matrix products also control gradient magnitude"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"Exploding values"}</th><td>{"Inputs, weights, update size, numeric precision"}</td><td>{"A bounded hidden function does not fix every upstream or output computation"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"Duplicate hidden features remain identical"}</th><td>{"Incoming and outgoing symmetry at initialization"}</td><td>{"Identical paths can receive identical updates"}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">{"A loaded model changes after an activation edit"}</th><td>{"Exact original function, approximation flag and parameters"}</td><td>{"Same tensor shapes do not imply the same model"}</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<Prose>{"Identical initialization is especially subtle. Picture a network whose two hidden neurons start with identical incoming weights and biases "}<em>{"and"}</em>{" identical outgoing weights to the output. Under the same deterministic update rule and optimizer state, they compute the same feature, play the same downstream role, receive the same gradient, and stay identical, so the network behaves as if it had one hidden feature. Our constructed XOR network already uses different hidden biases (0 and −1), so its two features are not identical. Even if the incoming weights and biases matched, different outgoing roles could produce different updates when the units have nonzero local slopes. Random initialization breaks this symmetry; that, not a lower starting loss, is its first job. Formal scale choices belong to Weight Initialization."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"Changing the activation of a pretrained model changes the function it computes. Preserve the expected activation and its approximation settings when reproducing a checkpoint; if you adapt them, evaluate the result as a changed model."}</Prose>

<H2>{"7. Deeper branches: smooth gates, expressive capacity and cost"}</H2>
<Prose>This branch extends the activation menu, connects §3's ramps to function approximation, and counts memory and parameters. Nothing in the next lesson depends on it.</Prose>
<H3>{"Smooth negative values and multiplicative gates"}</H3>
<Prose>{"ReLU sends every negative score to exactly 0, with slope 0. Leaky ReLU instead keeps a small slope "}<Math>{"\\alpha>0"}</Math>{" below zero, so negative scores still pass a weak signal:"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"\\phi(z)=\\begin{cases}z,&z\\ge 0\\\\ \\alpha z,&z<0.\\end{cases}"}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"At "}<Math>{"\\alpha=0.1"}</Math>{" and "}<Math>{"z=-2"}</Math>{", output is −0.2 and slope 0.1. ELU instead uses "}<Math>{"\\alpha(e^z-1)"}</Math>{" on its negative branch and "}<Math>{"z"}</Math>{" on its positive branch; for "}<Math>{"\\alpha=1"}</Math>{", it approaches −1 in the negative tail. Saturating negative values and a constant negative slope are different choices."}</Prose>
<Prose>Another way to read ReLU: it multiplies the score by a fraction that is either 0 (score negative) or 1 (score positive). A smooth alternative lets that fraction change continuously with the score. Keep two things separate: the <em>fraction retained</em>, between 0 and 1, and the <em>signed score being multiplied</em>. The bars show the fraction each function keeps at z = −1 and z = +1, and the number after each bar is the resulting output.</Prose>
<SmoothGateDecomposition />
<Prose>{"At a negative score, a positive fraction preserves a small negative output; it does not flip the sign. The formulas below say how each function chooses its fraction. GELU uses the standard-normal cumulative probability "}<Math>{"\\Phi(z)"}</Math>{": the area under a standard bell curve to the left of "}<Math>{"z"}</Math>{". That area is 0.5 at 0, about 0.84 at 1 and about 0.16 at −1, the bars above. So"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"\\operatorname{GELU}(z)=z\\Phi(z),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{GELU}'(z)=\\Phi(z)+z\\varphi(z),"}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"where "}<Math>{"\\varphi"}</Math>{" is the bell curve's height (the density). The slope has two parts because both factors change with "}<Math>{"z"}</Math>{": the product rule adds the fraction's value and the score times the fraction's own slope. The bell curve is only a smooth weighting rule; actual scores need not be normally distributed. The original motivation relates "}<Math>{"z\\Phi(z)"}</Math>{" to the expected value of an input-dependent random keep-or-drop mask, but the implemented GELU is deterministic. "}<a href="https://arxiv.org/html/1606.08415v5">{"GELU formulation, §2"}</a>{"."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"A common cheaper approximation is"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"\\frac z 2\\left[1+\\tanh\\left(\\sqrt{2/\\pi}(z+0.044715 z^3)\\right)\\right]."}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"Exact and approximate are distinct functions. At 1, their values are about 0.841345 and 0.841192. "}<a href="https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.14/generated/torch.nn.GELU.html">{"PyTorch's GELU documentation"}</a>{" exposes "}<Code>{"approximate=\"none\""}</Code>{" and "}<Code>{"\"tanh\""}</Code>{" explicitly."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"SiLU uses the sigmoid as its fraction: "}<Math>{"z\\sigma(z)"}</Math>{", also called Swish with fixed scale parameter 1; generalized Swish uses "}<Math>{"z\\sigma(\\beta z)"}</Math>{". Its derivative is "}<Math>{"\\sigma(z)+z\\sigma(z)(1-\\sigma(z))"}</Math>{", the same two-part product-rule shape. At −2, SiLU is approximately −0.2384 with derivative −0.0908. A negative input can therefore have a negative local slope; GELU also has a small negative-slope region. Smooth does not mean monotone, and neither function guarantees a nonzero derivative everywhere. "}<a href="https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.14/generated/torch.nn.SiLU.html">{"SiLU API"}</a>{", "}<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.05941">{"Swish paper"}</a>{"."}</Prose>
<NegativeSlopeFigure />
<Prose>Read the SiLU curve from the left. Increasing a negative input has two competing effects: the value becomes less negative, but the sigmoid fraction grows. Near −2, the growing fraction wins and the product becomes slightly more negative. That is the negative derivative, seen as the two terms of the product rule.</Prose>
<Prose>{"Mish is another smooth option, "}<Math>{"z\\tanh(\\operatorname{softplus}(z))"}</Math>{", where "}<Math>{"\\operatorname{softplus}(z)=\\log(1+e^z)"}</Math>{" should be computed with a stable library function. Read it with the same “value × smooth fraction” picture: softplus is a smooth version of the positive ramp, and tanh maps that positive quantity into (0, 1). At z = 0 the fraction is positive but the product is still zero. It is optional here rather than a seventh candidate silently added to the experiment."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"A "}<strong>{"gated layer"}</strong>{" goes one step further: the fraction is no longer computed from the same score, but by a separate learned projection. A "}<strong>{"projection"}</strong>{" here is simply a bias-free linear layer: multiply by a weight matrix to get a new vector. One projection produces values, the other produces gates that modulate them. The figure follows one SwiGLU block with two hidden coordinates."}</Prose>
<SwiGluFigure />
<Prose>{"Both branches read the same input. The gate branch's projected scores (1, −1) pass through SiLU, giving about (0.7311, −0.2689); the value branch's projected scores (2, 3) pass through no activation. The two are multiplied coordinate by coordinate, written "}<Math>{"\\odot"}</Math>{", giving about (1.4621, −0.8068), and a third projection maps the result back to the output width. This notation multiplies a row vector "}<Math>{"x"}</Math>{" on the left, so each weight matrix here has shape (input width, hidden width), the transpose of §3's layer matrix. A bias-free SwiGLU block is"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"h=\\operatorname{SiLU}(xW_g)\\odot(xW_v),\\qquad o=hW_o."}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"These gates are not bounded probabilities: SiLU of −1 is negative, so a gate can reverse the sign of a value, as the second coordinate shows."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"A complete small block in PyTorch. Each projection is an "}<Code>{"nn.Linear(..., bias=False)"}</Code>{", which stores its weight in §3's (outputs, inputs) layout. That stored matrix is the transpose of the "}<Math>{"W_g"}</Math>{", "}<Math>{"W_v"}</Math>{", "}<Math>{"W_o"}</Math>{" written above, and the layer computes "}<Math>{"xW^\\top"}</Math>{" with it."}</Prose>
<RunnableExample example={perceptronExamples[2]} />
<Prose>{"Expected shape is (2, 6), parameter count 144, and every output is 0 because all three projections have no bias and the input is zero. This is a shape, parameter-count and zero-input check, not a trained model."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"A plain two-projection feedforward block of input/output width "}<Math>{"d"}</Math>{" and hidden width "}<Math>{"h"}</Math>{" has "}<Math>{"2 dh"}</Math>{" weights, excluding biases. This gated block has "}<Math>{"3 dh_g"}</Math>{". An equal weight budget therefore uses "}<Math>{"h_g=2 h/3"}</Math>{". With "}<Math>{"d=6,h=12,h_g=8"}</Math>{", both have 144 weights. At the same hidden width, gating has 50% more weights. This budget adjustment is explicit in "}<a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2002.05202v1">{"Shazeer's GLU variants paper, §3.1"}</a>{"; gating is not a free improvement at an unchanged parameter count."}</Prose>
<H3>{"Many simple ramps can make a detailed function"}</H3>
<Prose>{"The XOR construction used a sum of ramps on the count "}<Math>{"s"}</Math>{". The same idea works for any continuous piecewise-linear function of one input: start with a line, and at each point where the slope should change, add a ramp that begins there and adds exactly the slope change. Three ramps make a triangular pulse:"}</Prose>
<TriangleFigure />
<Prose>{"The three panels are the ramps, and the fourth is their sum,"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"g(x)=\\operatorname{ReLU}(x)-2\\operatorname{ReLU}(x-1)+\\operatorname{ReLU}(x-2),"}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"which gives 0, 0.5, 1, 0.5, 0 at inputs 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2. The second panel is negative only because its weight is −2; every ReLU output is itself nonnegative. The slopes 0 → 1 → −1 → 0 need changes +1, −2 and +1, which are exactly the three coefficients. On the binary counts 0, 1, 2, the last ramp is zero and the first two reproduce §3's XOR output. In general, with breakpoints "}<Math>{"t_j"}</Math>{" and slope "}<Math>{"m_j"}</Math>{" immediately after each one,"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"f(x)=a+m_0 x+\\sum_j (m_j-m_{j-1})\\operatorname{ReLU}(x-t_j)."}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"Each ramp contributes nothing before its breakpoint, then changes the total slope. By adding breakpoints, piecewise-linear interpolation can approximate a continuous function on a bounded interval increasingly closely. Broader universal-approximation results concern networks of sufficient width, with biases and suitable nonpolynomial activations, approximating on bounded closed domains. “Nonlinear” alone is too weak a condition: polynomial activations in a fixed shallow architecture do not supply this universal family. These are existence theorems; they do not say that a chosen small network, dataset, optimizer and training budget will find the desired function. "}<a href="https://archive.nyu.edu/handle/2451/14329">{"Leshno and colleagues, working-paper statement and threshold condition"}</a>{"."}</Prose>
<H3>{"Count the actual tensor before estimating memory"}</H3>
<Prose>{"Language models apply the same layer at every token position of every example, so their activations carry an extra batch-like axis: the "}<strong>{"sequence length"}</strong>{", the number of token positions per example. A hidden activation tensor with batch 8, sequence length 4096 and hidden width 16384 contains"}</Prose>
<MathBlock>{"8\\cdot 4096\\cdot 16384=536{,}870{,}912"}</MathBlock>
<Prose>{"elements. At two bytes per element, its raw storage is 1,073,741,824 bytes, exactly 1 GiB. This is one tensor, not the total training footprint. Saved backward intermediates, additional gated branches, parameters, gradients, optimizer state and temporary kernels add storage; fusion or recomputation may avoid retaining some intermediates."}</Prose>
<Prose>{"For a block with input/output feature width d = 4096 and hidden width h = 16384, a plain bias-free two-projection block has 134,217,728 weights. The feature width d happens to equal the preceding sequence length, but they are separate tensor axes: parameter count depends on feature widths, not sequence length. Exact equal-budget gated width would be 10922⅔, which is not an integer. Rounding to 11008, an illustrative multiple of 256, gives 135,266,304 weights, slightly more. Hardware-friendly rounding is a configuration decision, not exact budget equality or a performance guarantee; benchmark the actual shapes if runtime matters. This lesson reports no measured speed ranking."}</Prose>
<H3>Deeper branch: check every activation against the library</H3>
<section id="perceptron-library-check">
<Prose>With all nine activations defined, one program can implement each of them and compare it with PyTorch. Its <Code>activation(name, z)</Code> extends §4's core with leaky ReLU, ELU, exact and approximate GELU, SiLU and Mish, returning values and slopes with the same shape as the input. It also repeats the <Code>nn.Linear</Code> layout check on a nonsymmetric matrix, follows the nonzero SwiGLU example through all three projections and the multiplication, and runs §2's scikit-learn perceptron comparison. Most of its output is agreement checks, which is its purpose: each line reports the largest difference from the library.</Prose>
<Prose>Numerical choices behind the formulas: the sigmoid slope uses exp(−|z|)/(1 + exp(−|z|))² rather than σ(1 − σ), which would subtract from a value already rounded to 1; Mish uses a stable softplus; exact GELU uses SciPy's vectorized complementary error function <Code>erfc</Code> to keep its small negative tail. ReLU and leaky ReLU use the stated framework conventions at zero. Differentiating the sum of outputs returns each local slope because the pointwise outputs are independent.</Prose>
<Prose>Environment and scope: Python 3.12 with the tested NumPy 2.3.5, SciPy 1.18.1, PyTorch 2.14.0 and scikit-learn 1.9.1; no data file is needed. Forward and slope differences were below 10⁻¹⁵ on the printed fixture, and the independent checks cover float64 scores from −40 to 40, shapes and zero conventions. The activation routines use O(K) work and temporary storage for K values; a dense N-by-D input projected to H outputs costs O(NDH) arithmetic and O(NH) output storage. Optimized framework kernels remain the normal choice for device execution and mixed precision.</Prose>
<CodeBlock language="bash">{'python -m pip install numpy==2.3.5 scipy==1.18.1 scikit-learn==1.9.1 torch==2.14.0\npython activation-mechanisms.py'}</CodeBlock>
<MechanismProgram {...mechanismProgram} title="Read and run the NumPy / library neuron comparison" />
<Prose><strong>Change the implementation:</strong> set the leaky negative slope to 0.2 on both sides and explain which scores change. Then change it on only one side and find a fixture that exposes the disagreement. Why does testing only positive inputs miss it?</Prose>
<details><summary>Implementation exercise solution</summary><Prose>At z = −2 the output becomes −0.4 and the local slope 0.2; positive scores keep their original output and slope. Zero keeps output zero while the chosen backward convention changes. A negative-score fixture distinguishes 0.1 from 0.2 immediately. Positive-only tests leave the edited branch unvisited.</Prose></details>
</section>
<section className="lesson-ending lesson-ending--practice" data-lesson-ending="practice"><H2>{"8. Practice: explain, compute, diagnose"}</H2>
<Prose>{"Try each problem before opening its hint or solution. Practices 9 and 10 belong to the first pass alongside 1–5."}</Prose>
<div className="lesson-exercise" data-lesson-exercise=""><H3>{"1. A changed boundary"}</H3>
<Prose>{"For "}<Math>{"w=(2,-1)"}</Math>{", "}<Math>{"b=-1"}</Math>{", classify "}<Math>{"x=(1,3)"}</Math>{" with the hard "}<Math>{"z>0"}</Math>{" rule. Find signed distance. Then multiply "}<Math>{"w"}</Math>{" and "}<Math>{"b"}</Math>{" by 3. Which results change?"}</Prose>
<details><summary>{"Hint"}</summary>
<Prose>{"Compute the two contributions before the bias. Distance divides the score by the weight-vector length."}</Prose>
</details>
<details><summary>{"Solution"}</summary>
<Prose>{"Score is 2 − 3 − 1 = −2, so the output is 0. Distance is "}<Math>{"-2/\\sqrt 5\\approx-0.8944"}</Math>{". Rescaling gives score −6 and weight length "}<Math>{"3\\sqrt 5"}</Math>{", so distance and hard output stay unchanged. Sigmoid decreases from about 0.1192 to 0.00247. Rescaling confidence is not moving the boundary."}</Prose>
</details></div>
<div className="lesson-exercise" data-lesson-exercise=""><H3>{"2. Update the model, then check a different example"}</H3>
<Prose>{"Start "}<Math>{"w=(0,0),b=0"}</Math>{". Present "}<Math>{"x=(2,-1)"}</Math>{" with label +1 and step size 0.5. What changes? What score does the updated model assign to the previously unseen point (0, 2)?"}</Prose>
<details><summary>{"Hint"}</summary>
<Prose>{"The current margin is 0, so the rule updates. Include the bias update."}</Prose>
</details>
<details><summary>{"Solution"}</summary>
<Prose>{"The new weights are (1, −0.5), and bias 0.5. The training point's score is 3; the unseen point's score is −0.5. Improving the presented example is not a claim that every other point becomes positive or correct."}</Prose>
</details></div>
<div className="lesson-exercise" data-lesson-exercise=""><H3>{"3. Repair a shifted XOR feature"}</H3>
<Prose>{"Keep "}<Math>{"h_1=\\max(0,x_1+x_2)"}</Math>{", but replace "}<Math>{"h_2"}</Math>{" by "}<Math>{"\\max(0,x_1+x_2-0.5)"}</Math>{". Can an output "}<Math>{"q=h_1+vh_2"}</Math>{" match all four XOR labels by changing only "}<Math>{"v"}</Math>{"?"}</Prose>
<details><summary>{"Hint"}</summary>
<Prose>{"Write the equations for sums 1 and 2. They must use the same "}<Math>{"v"}</Math>{"."}</Prose>
</details>
<details><summary>{"Solution"}</summary>
<Prose>{"At sum 1, matching 1 requires "}<Math>{"1+0.5 v=1"}</Math>{", so "}<Math>{"v=0"}</Math>{". At sum 2, matching 0 requires "}<Math>{"2+1.5 v=0"}</Math>{", so "}<Math>{"v=-4/3"}</Math>{". No single value satisfies both. Repairing one highlighted row is insufficient; change the hidden bias or allow other parameters to change."}</Prose>
</details></div>
<div className="lesson-exercise" data-lesson-exercise=""><H3>{"4. Follow tensor shapes"}</H3>
<Prose>{"A network receives 7 examples with 12 features, uses 5 hidden neurons, and outputs 3 logits. Give both weight shapes, both bias shapes, and the number of learned scalars."}</Prose>
<details><summary>{"Hint"}</summary>
<Prose>{"Use the PyTorch convention: output features first in a weight matrix."}</Prose>
</details>
<details><summary>{"Solution"}</summary>
<Prose>{"Weights are 5×12 and 3×5; biases are 5 and 3. Total is 60+5+15+3=83. Intermediate batches are 7×5 and 7×3. The batch size changes computation and activation storage, not the parameter count."}</Prose>
</details></div>
<div className="lesson-exercise" data-lesson-exercise=""><H3>{"5. Diagnose an activation claim"}</H3>
<Prose>{"A colleague says, “ReLU has derivative 1, so a ten-layer positive scalar ReLU chain cannot shrink gradients.” Each affine weight in the chain is 0.5. What is the input sensitivity? What extra fact would you need for a real vector network?"}</Prose>
<details><summary>{"Hint"}</summary>
<Prose>{"Multiply the weight and activation slope at each layer."}</Prose>
</details>
<details><summary>{"Solution"}</summary>
<Prose>{"Along this all-positive path, the product is "}<Math>{"0.5^{10}=1/1024"}</Math>{". The activation contributes 1 each time, but the weights shrink the signal. For a vector network, use the actual weight matrices, activation masks and directions; a single scalar derivative slogan is insufficient."}</Prose>
</details></div>
<div className="lesson-exercise" data-lesson-exercise=""><H3>{"6. Allocate a gated width"}</H3>
<Prose>{"A plain bias-free block uses width 24 and hidden width 60. Find an exactly equal-weight SwiGLU hidden width and both counts."}</Prose>
<details><summary>{"Hint"}</summary>
<Prose>{"Compare two projections with three projections."}</Prose>
</details>
<details><summary>{"Solution"}</summary>
<Prose>{"Plain count is "}<Math>{"2(24)(60)=2880"}</Math>{". Gated width 40 gives "}<Math>{"3(24)(40)=2880"}</Math>{". Keeping hidden width 60 would give 4320, not an equal-budget comparison. Biases or differently shaped projections would require a new count."}</Prose>
</details></div>
<div className="lesson-exercise" data-lesson-exercise=""><H3>{"7. Build a tent somewhere else"}</H3>
<Prose>{"Construct a piecewise-linear pulse that is 0 at 0 and 2, reaches 2 at 1, and stays 0 outside [0, 2]. Use three ReLUs and verify at −1, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 3."}</Prose>
<details><summary>{"Hint"}</summary>
<Prose>{"Scale the triangle in §7. Its slope changes at 0, 1 and 2."}</Prose>
</details>
<details><summary>{"Solution"}</summary>
<Prose><Math>{"2\\operatorname{ReLU}(x)-4\\operatorname{ReLU}(x-1)+2\\operatorname{ReLU}(x-2)"}</Math>{" gives 0, 1, 2, 1, 0 at those inputs. The last term restores the slope to 0 beyond 2; omitting it would create a descending line, not a bounded pulse."}</Prose>
</details></div>
<div className="lesson-exercise" data-lesson-exercise=""><H3>{"8. Extend the experiment without rewriting its conclusion"}</H3>
<Prose>{"Change hidden width 32 to 8 for all six activations, keep the split and seeds, and report training losses and paired validation errors. Explain which differences the controlled comparison can resolve. What would count as a supported conclusion?"}</Prose>
<details><summary>{"Hint"}</summary>
<Prose>{"The experiment changes capacity and keeps a single learning-rate protocol. It still has no untouched final test."}</Prose>
</details>
<details><summary>{"Solution and evaluation criteria"}</summary>
<Prose>{"A good report identifies the changed width, all three seeds, all candidates, training loss and validation denominators. It compares which image IDs were repaired or broken, and distinguishes the proposed explanation from the observed result. A supported conclusion is limited to this width, dataset and protocol; it may report ties or a reversed comparison. Reporting only the winning seed, labeling validation as test, or claiming that one activation is always superior fails the task. There is no prewritten accuracy result for this unexecuted extension."}</Prose>
</details></div>
<div className="lesson-exercise" data-lesson-exercise=""><H3>{"9. Train OR by hand"}</H3>
<Prose>{"OR has labels −1, +1, +1, +1 for corners 00, 01, 10, 11. Using §2's rule (zero start, step size 1, that corner order, update when the margin is ≤ 0), compute the coefficients "}<Math>{"(w_1,w_2,b)"}</Math>{" after each presentation of the first epoch, and the update count of the second epoch. Then check every step in Investigation C with the OR task."}</Prose>
<details><summary>{"Hint"}</summary>
<Prose>{"Append 1 to each corner. A tie (score 0) updates in either direction, depending on the label."}</Prose>
</details>
<details><summary>{"Solution"}</summary>
<Prose>{"Epoch 1: 00 scores 0 → subtract (0, 0, 1) → (0, 0, −1). 01 scores −1 with label +1 → add (0, 1, 1) → (0, 1, 0). 10 scores 0 → add (1, 0, 1) → (1, 1, 1). 11 scores 3, margin 3 → no change. Epoch 1 ends at (1, 1, 1) with 3 updates. Epoch 2: 00 now scores 1 with label −1, a mistake → (1, 1, 0); the other three scores 1, 1 and 2 are positive, so epoch 2 makes 1 update. Training eventually stops at (2, 2, −1) after epoch 6, because OR is separable."}</Prose>
</details></div>
<div className="lesson-exercise" data-lesson-exercise=""><H3>{"10. Reuse the hidden space for a different task"}</H3>
<Prose>{"XNOR outputs 1 when the two inputs are equal: 1, 0, 0, 1 for corners 00, 01, 10, 11. Keep §3's hidden features "}<Math>{"h_1,h_2"}</Math>{" unchanged. Find an output "}<Math>{"q'=\\alpha h_1+\\beta h_2+c"}</Math>{" that matches all four targets exactly, and describe the separating line in the hidden-space figure."}</Prose>
<details><summary>{"Hint"}</summary>
<Prose>{"There are only three distinct hidden points: (0, 0), (1, 0) and (2, 1). Write one equation for each."}</Prose>
</details>
<details><summary>{"Solution"}</summary>
<Prose><Math>{"(0,0)"}</Math>{" needs "}<Math>{"c=1"}</Math>{"; "}<Math>{"(1,0)"}</Math>{" needs "}<Math>{"\\alpha+c=0"}</Math>{", so "}<Math>{"\\alpha=-1"}</Math>{"; "}<Math>{"(2,1)"}</Math>{" needs "}<Math>{"-2+\\beta+1=1"}</Math>{", so "}<Math>{"\\beta=2"}</Math>{". Thus "}<Math>{"q'=1-h_1+2h_2=1-q"}</Math>{". The same hidden line separates the points; only the output neuron's sign and offset change. A useful hidden representation can serve several output tasks, which is the seed of transfer learning later in this module."}</Prose>
</details></div></section>
<section className="lesson-ending lesson-ending--resources" data-lesson-ending="resources"><H2>{"9. References and another way to learn"}</H2>
<ul><li><a href="https://www.3blue1brown.com/lessons/neural-networks/">{"3Blue1Brown: But what is a Neural Network?"}</a>{" — creator-hosted video with a substantial text companion. Use it after §1: its picture of one neuron's weights drawn as a pixel pattern is the 64-pixel version of Investigation A's template. The text companion was reviewed; the video was not independently watched. Its bounded-neuron imagery is especially natural for sigmoid, while our ReLU outputs can exceed 1."}</li><li><a href="http://neuralnetworksanddeeplearning.com/chap1.html">{"Michael Nielsen, Neural Networks and Deep Learning, chapter 1"}</a>{" — free online book. Its perceptron and sigmoid-neuron sections explain, from the other direction, why a step function makes small weight changes useless for learning (§4 here). Consulted through search excerpts of those passages; read it as an alternate narrative, not as an extra requirement."}</li><li><a href="https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs4780/2022sp/notes/LectureNotes06.html">{"Cornell CS 4780: Perceptron"}</a>{" — lecture notes with accompanying videos. Use after §2 for bias augmentation and the convergence proof; it is an alternate mathematical route, not required background."}</li><li><a href="https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/mlp.html">{"Deep Learning, chapter 6: Deep Feedforward Networks"}</a>{" — textbook route through XOR, hidden units and architecture. Its XOR example uses the same two ReLU features as §3 and describes the hidden layer as a learned representation in which a linear model suffices. Start with that section, and save its backpropagation section for the next lesson."}</li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/1606.08415v5">{"GELU paper"}</a>{" and "}<a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2002.05202v1">{"GLU Variants Improve Transformer"}</a>{" — original definitions and particular experiments. Read the formulation and parameter-budget sections before interpreting experimental rankings."}</li><li><a href="https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.14/generated/torch.nn.Linear.html">{"PyTorch Linear"}</a>{", "}<a href="https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.14/generated/torch.nn.GELU.html">{"GELU"}</a>{", and "}<a href="https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.14/generated/torch.nn.SiLU.html">{"SiLU"}</a>{" — exact shape and function contracts used by the programs."}</li><li><a href="https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/80/optical+recognition+of+handwritten+">{"UCI Optical Recognition of Handwritten Digits"}</a>{" — E. Alpaydin and C. Kaynak, 1998, CC BY 4.0, DOI 10.24432/C50P49. The accompanying provenance describes our subset and its limitations."}</li></ul>
<Prose>{"You can now trace a network forward, say why a single neuron draws one straight boundary, and separate what a network can express from how it learns. The next topic, "}<strong>{"Backpropagation & Automatic Differentiation"}</strong>{", starts from one ReLU neuron and a squared-error loss, and turns §4's local slopes into the gradient of every parameter by following the loss backward through the same operations."}</Prose></section>
 </div>
};
export default lesson;
